WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.320
What do humans,
some primates, some bats,

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:06.920
the spiny mouse, and the elephant
shrew have in common?

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:09.400
They all get periods.

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:10.800
I'm Gabriella Kountourides,

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:13.800
and I'm studying for my doctorate
at the University of Oxford.

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:16.720
And I am fascinated
about menstrual cycles.

00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:18.920
Menstruation is a natural process
that affects

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:21.440
half of the world's population
during their lives,

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:23.000
but it's little understood.

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:27.360
So here's five things you probably
didn't know about periods.

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:30.760
First, some myth-busting.

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:34.000
Moon syncing is not a thing.

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:35.600
It's such a compelling idea,

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:39.520
the idea that menstrual cycles
sync up with the cyclical waxing

00:00:39.560 --> 00:00:42.720
and waning of the moon,
much like the ocean tides.

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:45.720
The moon's cycle is 29.5 days,

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:50.160
but in 2019, an analysis of
7.5 million menstrual cycles

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:53.280
from 1.5 million women
around the world

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:55.280
found no relationship at all.

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:58.680
Another commonly-held view
is that when people live together,

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:01.760
their periods sync up.
Humans love to find patterns,

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:05.000
and a certain amount of overlap
is to be expected.

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:08.520
But actual syncing of cycles
is not backed by data.

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:12.320
The textbook idea of a 28-day cycle
is a bit of a myth too.

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:14.920
A normal cycle can be anything up to
seven days

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:17.960
longer or shorter than this.
Everyone is unique.

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:20.840
Hormones can be powerful...
in a good way.

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:24.440
OK. Let's talk about oestrogen.

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:26.600
It's an incredible hormone

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:28.800
that we're only just starting to
understand.

00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:31.600
There's a specific type
of oestrogen that's

00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:34.160
made in the ovaries
called estradiol.

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:36.640
This is released
throughout the menstrual cycle,

00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:38.880
and it peaks just around ovulation.

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:42.600
Research suggests oestrogen can have
some powerful effects.

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:44.560
In 2021, neuroscientists

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:47.520
at the University of California,
Santa Barbara,

00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:50.680
for the first time tracked a woman's
hormones throughout the cycle

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:54.080
and looked at whether these had
any impact on the brain.

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:56.200
They found an increase
in connectivity

00:01:56.240 --> 00:01:59.520
throughout the brain at times
when oestrogen was high.

00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:02.920
One theory is that oestrogen
causes our neurons

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:05.320
to form new connections
to other neurons,

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:08.120
which helps with
learning and memory.

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:11.880
98% of mammals don't have periods.

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:14.320
Lots of animals do have
reproductive cycles,

00:02:14.360 --> 00:02:17.080
like cats and elephants,
but they reabsorb

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:20.360
the lining of the womb rather than
shedding it as a period.

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:22.520
So why do humans menstruate?

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:24.800
The truth is, we don't know.
One theory,

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:28.400
put forward by evolutionary
geneticist Deena Emera,

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:32.760
is that it's a tug-of-war of energy
between the mother and baby.

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:35.560
Human embryos bury deep
into the lining of the womb,

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:39.320
which gives them more access to
nutrients from the mother's body.

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:42.560
So the lining of the womb builds up
nice and thick each month

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:45.040
to create a shield
to protect the mother.

00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:48.240
This thick lining also makes it
easier to shed, so she doesn't

00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:52.160
expend a huge amount of energy if
a foetus won't ultimately make it.

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:58.040
The ancient Greeks thought the womb
wandered around the body, literally.

00:02:58.080 --> 00:02:59.480
And as it floated around,

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:03.440
they believed this wandering womb
caused all sorts of diseases.

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:07.200
Bizarre as it sounds, this idea
stuck around for centuries,

00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:09.560
and so did the idea of hysteria -

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:12.240
a kind of catch-all
pseudo-medical term

00:03:12.280 --> 00:03:14.400
which was used to undermine women.

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:21.960
In 1931, a new term was coined -
PMT, or premenstrual tension.

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:25.960
This was later renamed
premenstrual syndrome or PMS,

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:28.240
and refers to any symptoms
experienced

00:03:28.280 --> 00:03:30.520
in the week before the start
of a period.

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:33.400
Some argue the term "syndrome"
is not useful

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:36.840
and it would be more accurate
to refer to premenstrual symptoms.

00:03:38.840 --> 00:03:41.400
PMS might be linked to inflammation.

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:43.960
Why do women experience
premenstrual symptoms?

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:46.600
It could be down to inflammation.

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:50.520
Inflammation is the body's way
of protecting itself from infection,

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:53.800
and just as our hormone levels
change during a menstrual cycle,

00:03:53.840 --> 00:03:55.680
so do levels of inflammation.

00:03:55.720 --> 00:03:58.040
At ovulation,
part of our immune system

00:03:58.080 --> 00:03:59.960
is temporarily suppressed
a little.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:02.240
That's so a fertilised egg,
if there is one,

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:04.800
can implant itself without
getting rejected.

00:04:04.840 --> 00:04:08.000
While inflammation is a sign
of a strong immune system,

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:10.000
too much inflammation is bad.

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:12.480
Symptoms include headaches,
tiredness,

00:04:12.520 --> 00:04:16.560
changes in appetite -
sounds a bit like PMS, doesn't it?

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:19.160
Some have suggested it's this rise
in inflammation

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:21.320
in the second half of the cycle

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:24.280
that's actually behind
what we call PMS.

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:26.640
So that's a quick rundown
of a few facts,

00:04:26.680 --> 00:04:29.040
myths and theories about periods.

00:04:29.080 --> 00:04:32.920
And still, there's so much unknown
about something that happens

00:04:32.960 --> 00:04:35.600
to half of the world's population
at some point in their life.

00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:37.320
But that's exciting,
because it means

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:39.280
there is so much more to discover.

